{"id":733,"date":"2026-03-07T09:26:14","date_gmt":"2026-03-07T09:26:14","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:10005\/?p=733"},"modified":"2026-03-08T07:30:41","modified_gmt":"2026-03-08T07:30:41","slug":"733-2","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/en\/733-2\/","title":{"rendered":"RIS-Assisted Secret Key Generation Method under Multi-Antenna Spatially Correlated Channels"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">RIS-Assisted Secret Key Generation Method under Multi-Antenna Spatially Correlated Channels<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">L. Hu, G. Li, X. Qian, A. Hu, and D. W. K. Ng<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>IEEE Transactions on Wireless Communications<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons lab-btn-group is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/Reconfigurable_Intelligent_Surface-Assisted_Secret_Key_Generation_in_Spatially_Correlated_Channels.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">\ud83d\udcc4 PDF<\/a><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-fill\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/TWC.2023.3296076\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">\ud83d\udd17 DOI Link<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p>Reconfigurable Intelligent Surfaces (RIS), with their ability to intelligently customize the wireless propagation environment, are a disruptive technology for enhancing Physical Layer Security secret key generation performance. Existing RIS-assisted PKG methods mostly rely on the idealized assumption of independent and identically distributed channels at the base station and RIS, which is inaccurate for typical RIS in isotropic scattering environments; neglecting channel spatial correlation may lead to degraded PKG performance. This paper establishes a general spatially correlated channel model and proposes a novel channel probing framework based on transmit beamforming and reflective beamforming. We derive closed-form expressions for the secret key generation rate and formulate an optimization problem, solved by a low-complexity block successive upper bound minimization combined with the Mirror-Prox method. Simulation results show that, compared to existing methods based on the i.i.d. fading model, the proposed method achieves approximately 5 dB transmit power gain when the spacing between adjacent RIS elements is a quarter wavelength; furthermore, KGR improves significantly with an increasing number of RIS elements, while only marginal gains are observed with increasing numbers of BS and user antennas.<\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Core Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Establishing an RIS-assisted PKG framework under spatially correlated channels<\/strong>: For MIMOME systems with spatial correlation at both the BS and RIS, a PKG framework based on transmit beamforming and reflective beamforming is proposed, closed-form expressions for KGR are derived, and an optimization problem maximizing the minimum KGR under the worst-case eavesdropper condition is formulated; theoretical analysis proves that beamforming performance under the correlated channel model outperforms the i.i.d. assumption, and the KGR gain increases with channel correlation.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Proposing a low-complexity BSUM + Mirror-Prox algorithm<\/strong>: The non-convex optimization problem is reformulated as an equivalent convex-concave saddle point problem, solved via the Mirror-Prox method with closed-form updates, and the monotonic non-decreasing convergence of the BSUM algorithm is proven; compared to the commonly used ASSG algorithm (alternating optimization + semidefinite relaxation + Gaussian randomization), the computation time is reduced by approximately two orders of magnitude while maintaining comparable KGR performance.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Revealing the dual impact of spatial correlation on KGR<\/strong>: Both theory and simulations demonstrate that BS antenna correlation and RIS element correlation positively contribute to KGR\u2014the smaller the element spacing and the stronger the correlation, the more significant the KGR improvement; meanwhile, as the numbers of BS and user antennas increase, KGR exhibits a marginal diminishing trend due to channel hardening effects, indicating that the number of RIS elements is a more critical factor for enhancing PKG performance.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-b46d2053 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-49-17.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-49-17.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-49-17.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-49-17.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-910\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-07_21-49-17\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">The proposed framework operates in a MIMOME system, where Alice (BS) and Bob (user) alternately probe the channel via a TDD protocol with RIS assistance, while countering the multi-antenna eavesdropper Eve.<strong>Downlink channel probing phase<\/strong>, Alice transmits pilots using optimized transmit beamforming vectors <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\mathbf{w}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> , and Bob performs combining and LS channel estimation on the received signals;<strong>Uplink channel probing phase<\/strong>, Bob transmits pilots, and Alice similarly completes channel estimation. The RIS reflective beamforming matrix <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">\u03a6<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol{\\Phi}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> and Alice's transmit beamforming vectors <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\mathbf{w}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> are jointly optimized to maximize the minimum KGR under the worst-case eavesdropping channel condition, and the resulting channel estimates undergo quantization, information reconciliation, and privacy amplification to generate symmetric keys.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-a3b04d11 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-536919e5 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-51-22.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-51-22.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-51-22.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_21-51-22.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-911\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-07_21-51-22\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Approximately 5 dB transmit power gain and about two orders of magnitude reduction in computational complexity achieved by considering spatial correlation<\/strong>: When <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>\u2265<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">P \\geq 30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, the spacing between adjacent RIS elements is <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>\u03bb<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\lambda\/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, and the BS antenna correlation coefficient <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>\u03c1<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\rho = 0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> , the proposed scheme achieves approximately 5 dB transmit power gain over beamforming schemes based on the i.i.d. assumption, and approximately 6 dB gain over the optimal transmit beamforming scheme without RIS; meanwhile, the computation time of the BSUM + Mirror-Prox algorithm is reduced by about two orders of magnitude compared to the ASSG algorithm while maintaining comparable KGR performance.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Citation<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@article{hu2024ris,\n  author    = {Lei Hu and Guyue Li and Xuewen Qian and Aiqun Hu and Derrick Wing Kwan Ng},\n  title     = {Reconfigurable Intelligent Surface-Assisted Secret Key Generation in Spatially Correlated Channels},\n  journal   = {IEEE Transactions on Wireless Communications},\n  volume    = {23},\n  number    = {3},\n  pages     = {2153--2166},\n  month     = mar,\n  year      = {2024},\n  doi       = {10.1109\/TWC.2023.3296076}\n}\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-content-justification-space-between is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-788841da wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Reconfigurable Intelligent Surface (RIS), with its intelligent customization capability for wireless propagation environments, is a disruptive technology for enhancing the performance of Physical Layer Secret Key Generation (PKG). This paper establishes a general spatially correlated channel model and proposes a novel channel probing framework based on transmit beamforming and reflective beamforming.<\/p>","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":910,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"mypost","format":"standard","meta":{"_uag_custom_page_level_css":"","footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-733","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","ha